hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in her essay  trying out one's new sword mary Midgley  coins a term moral isolationism that she  uses to describe a certain type of  relativistic position in ethics and  she's primarily focused on a kind of  cultural relativism but this could be  applied much more broadly to other  varieties of ethical relativism for  example those that might have to do with  race class gender within a society or to  saying you can't understand me and judge  me because you don't come from the same  background or have the same experiences  as I do and we'll talk about that and  those implications a bit later for right  now let's stick with what she's talking  about so what is what she's calling  moral isolationism  it's a standpoint ethical perspective on  the possibility of having ethical  perspectives about those who are  different those who are other those who  belong to another culture and she says  that in our own day we're hearing about  other people's cultures and customs and  activities we see changes in our  lifetime which would have astonished our  parents and I'm going to discuss one  very short way of dealing with this  difficulty a drastic way  which many people now theoretically  favor and this is what she's calling  moral isolationism and she tells us  about some of its key features so what  are these she says it consists in simply  denying we can ever understand any  culture except our own well enough to  make judgments about it so we can't  praise condemn justify explain any other  cultures we can only understand our own  culture we can only understand the  culture that we either were born into or  were adopted into and we're raised  within or perhaps even had imposed upon  us if we lost the culture that we  originated from in the first place and  we're sort of brainwashed into that we  get one and that's it so whatever it is  that you're working with that's your one  culture it's impossible from this point  of view to be the cultural equivalent of  bilingual you cannot straddle multiple  cultures you wouldn't be able to do for  example what they call code-switching in  language using the language of one  culture as opposed to another culture or  rather subculture that would be ruled  out as a possibility because you can  only understand one so we can only  understand our own culture why because  as she puts it societies or something  like hermetically sealed off units with  with each with its own system of thought  it has its own material implements which  we can see at museums or in other places  like National Geographic you know or  whatever other things that you're  getting popular anthropology in and each  one of them is radically different from  the other they may borrow things from  each other but the same thing within a  different culture has a totally  different meaning that we can't possibly  fathom every single culture is its own  world unto itself  a third very important part of this as  she says is wonderful metaphor  that moral judgment is a kind of coinage  valid only in its country of origin so  moral judgment would be saying things  are good or bad right or wrong  better or worse desirable undesirable  the sorts of things that we do in  praising blaming criticizing justifying  suggesting all of that would be possible  only from within the standpoint of one's  own culture so if this is really the  case that implies that unless we have  some radical continuity between say us  and ancient Athens we can't even  understand the moral perspective of  Socrates or the moral perspective of the  ancient Israelites in the Bible or the  moral perspective of Rome let alone the  moral perspective of 16th century China  all of those are sealed off containers  any moral judgments that were made are  only valid within those systems and so  along with this goes the implication  that we since we cannot understand we  can't make judgments about and we can't  even morally reason about other cultures  their practices their works their  justifications their social structures  none of that is things that we could  understand and then judge and then  reason about we can't say whether  they're better or worse we can't compare  them with each other they simply are  what they are for the people who are  living with them and for everybody else  it's a matter of hands off now that's a  very strong relativist position it's not  just saying that different people have  different moral codes it's saying we  literally cannot make sense of the moral  code of a different culture so we cannot  evaluate it in any way whatsoever or use  it as a point of comparison and contrast  either with their own culture or with  other cultures why might a person take a  position  like that so she brings up three main  things one is that within the culture  that we inhabit late modern Western  culture she's writing from a British  standpoint but this could equally apply  to Americans or pretty much anybody in  the first world we tend to value respect  and tolerance for other cultures that is  when we're not engaging saying cultural  appropriation or making fun of them at  racist ways or doing things like that  but there are many of us who do think  that it's important to respect other  cultures to show tolerance you know you  see somebody doing something that looks  a little bit unusual that that you can  just say well they're they're from a  different place what they're doing has a  different meaning to them I'll give you  a great example at a very very fancy  dinner one of the guests of honor who is  from the American West and grew up on  ranches took the fancy dinner napkin and  blew her nose in it to the astonished  and in some cases disgusted stares of  all the onlookers well at least some of  the onlookers because some of us were  like well you know that's what napkins  are used for in other places that would  be showing a kind of Tolerance or a  respect for that culture and instead  quite a few of the other people took  offense to that and thought that it was  very wrong to do the napkins all get  washed in the same washing machine as  everything else  another thing that she talks about is  concerns about the the opposite about  people despising other cultures saying  terrible things about them look at those  barbarians look at those dirty savages  look at those people over there who do  this thing you know  or it could be joking about them it  could be all sorts of things caricatures  oppressing other cultures steamroller to  use her her term other cultures maybe  the idea is that if we adopt this moral  isolationism we don't try to change  everybody to make them like us which in  fact was something that did a  her prime example of that is the  indoctrination of many people from  different Native American tribes for  generations here in the United States  very often in church run schools they  were going to make good Westerners good  Americans out of these these Native  Americans or First Peoples and in effect  they they managed to you know  deracinated them their own culture  produce all sorts of you know bad  emotional effects and complexes and  didn't give them what they really needed  to to succeed in can in the white  culture of the time where they probably  wouldn't have succeeded anyway so maybe  you know if we're tuned to those sorts  of problems we might adopt something  like moral isolationism let people live  over here and live the way that they  want you don't try to indoctrinate them  don't try to remove their culture don't  try to destroy it one of the reasons why  Midgley at the end talks about this  becoming sort of a paradigm is because  we look at the fruits of anthropology as  if they are showing us human nature as  such so she tells us that the universal  predicament of culture which is that  cultures borrow from each other all the  time has been obscured by the fact that  anthropologists used to concentrate  largely on very small and remote  cultures which didn't seem to have this  problem of cultural borrowing these tiny  societies which had often forgotten  their own history made neat  self-contained subjects for study and  she goes on and says no doubt it was  valuable to emphasize the remoteness  their strangeness their independence of  our cultural tradition this emphasis was  I think the root of moral isolationism  and so that gives you an idea of why  somebody might adopt this sort of  standpoint now there are some important  consequences to adopting this standpoint  there there's two of them that she  highlights in general in addition to  pointing out a lot of different problems  that come up as well so one is that as I  pointed out  the moral isolationism as she says has a  general ban on moral reasoning it rules  it out across the board except within  your own framework except within your  own culture now what do we mean by moral  reasoning she says here's just an  example the power of moral judgment is  not a luxury not a perverse indulgence  of the self-righteous it is a necessity  when we judge something to be bad or  good better or worse than something else  we are taking it as an example to aim  that or avoid without opinions of this  sort we would have no framework of  comparison for our own policy  we could form no judgments on our own  actions and she talks a little bit about  Homo sapiens and our brains and how this  is actually part of our very nature  making moral judgments  so moral isolationism would rule out  most moral judgments that we would make  insofar as they involve any sort of  reference to anything outside of our own  culture that would be a significant  problem the other thing that she points  out is logically this sort of position  should in fact lead to what she calls  moral skepticism which would mean that  we would engage in no action at all  because we wouldn't be able to judge it  one way or another as good or bad we  wouldn't be able to carry out practical  reasoning and we wouldn't have an  interest in other cultures or perhaps  even in much of our own and this leads  us to a very important point if you  think about a culture if we talk about  something like the West is the West  really a culture or is it an incredibly  complex network of overlapping cultures  some of which have more dominance than  others how do we treat things like this  within our own society do we say it  matters what race one is classified as  within the the country that one lives in  because of course we know that race as a  socially constructed matter and not  merely as genetic correlates to those  social constructs is very arbitrary  changes from place to place culture to  culture designation to designation  generation to generation if we adopt a  sort of standpoint that says because I'm  not of that race  I can't possibly judge what goes on in  that community I can't even understand  it and they you know may tell me that or  other people on the side and I said who  are you to judge or anything like that  then we're adopting a position of moral  isolationism when we say listen you  can't possibly know what I'm going  through because I have a different  sexual orientation than you I'm a  different gender I come from a different  part of the country you know you're from  the coast I'm from flyover country I'm  from a rural area you're from an urban  area well I grew up in the suburbs you  know I moved around a lot on different  military bases when we say that we can't  understand and therefore can't engage in  any moral reasoning or judgment about  the actions the beliefs the statements  of others because they belong to a  different group were in effect going  into something like Midgley's moral  isolationism that she's pointing out the  problems with here so her conception of  moral isolationism doesn't just apply to  cultural relativism it can apply to  ethical relativism much more broadly  anytime that we have some sort of  determining quality or group or  characteristic that is supposed to act  as a ban on understanding altogether so  that is what Midgley means by moral  isolationism and that is its connection  with relativism  you
 

